<p>Alice Josephine Davis — known to her family and friends throughout her life as Jo, was born in 1904 in the small mountain town of Alma, Park County, Colorado, the daughter of Robert Davis and Anna Maud Woodruff, and so a granddaughter, on her mother's side, of John Farney Woodruff and Annie E. Chaney. Her childhood moved through several of Colorado's mining towns: the 1910 census places the family in Florissant, Teller County, and a postcard her mother sent home in December 1913 puts them near St. Elmo, in the Chalk Creek country outside Buena Vista. By 1918, when Alice Josephine was fourteen, the family had moved on again, this time to Mineral County, settling in Creede — the same year her teenage brother, Robert Lincoln Davis, died in the influenza pandemic. Two years later, when Alice Josephine was sixteen, her parents' marriage ended in divorce, filed in that same county; her father left Colorado for Missouri not long after, and her mother remarried within weeks.</p>

<p>Alice Josephine herself married in 1921, at seventeen, a quiet wedding on Tuesday, July 5th, at her mother's home, performed by County Judge Clarence Y. Butler and witnessed by her own mother, Anna Maud, and by Mrs. A. M. Davis. Her husband, William Thomas Jackson, was a Creede-raised boy known to everyone as Billy; the local paper, reporting the marriage under the headline "Billy and Joe," noted that the bride was known to her own friends as "Joe." The couple remained in Creede, raising four children through the 1920s and 1930s - Joel, William Charles ("Scoot"), John Richard, and a daughter still living today. It was a marriage that knit together the family's two Mineral County threads: her own parents had divorced in that same county the year before, and Billy's family had mined and raised children in its camps for a generation before him.</p>

<p>Billy died in 1957, after thirty-six years of marriage. Alice Josephine wrote a poem afterward, "<a href="/writings/the-empty-room/">The Empty Room</a>," which their son John later published under his own column heading, "Marriages Weather Storms with Love."</p>

<p>She stayed on in Creede as a widow for more than twenty years. In July 1974 she signed a will there, leaving her father's Roosevelt-signed postmaster appointment and the family Bible to her brother Arthur, her gemstones and Indian artifacts to Joel, her typewriter and papers to John. It asked that she be cremated as soon as practicable and said plainly, "I do not want any type of funeral service and request no flowers." She died on 7 July 1980 at Penrose Hospital in Colorado Springs, at seventy-six, of complications from a cancer that had begun about two and a half years earlier, and was cremated the next day. Her ashes, by the family's account, now rest in Creede Cemetery beneath a stone her children raised for both of their parents.</p>